> 
> | Person | What to know / good conversation hooks |
> |---|---|
> | **Jeffrey Sun** | Likely your local organizer/host contact. If he’s a grad student or staff organizer, prioritize logistics gratitude and ask who in the department is most engaged with climate/environment work. Good person for “who should I follow up with?” |
> | **Jacquelyn Pless — Rotman School of Management** | Energy/environmental economist at Rotman; works around clean-energy innovation, electricity markets, policy design, and firm/technology responses to incentives. Strong overlap with your climate damages work via adaptation, mitigation incentives, and translating evidence for business/policy audiences. Ask about innovation bottlenecks and how firms internalize climate risk. |
> | **Christian Spielmann** | UofT economics visitor/faculty meeting; I’m less certain on his exact field from memory. Go in with a broad opener: “What are you working on lately?” Then connect depending on whether he leans macro, trade, development, or environmental. |
> | **Karen Clay** | Major economic historian/environmental economist, known for work on pollution, energy, public health, and long-run environmental regulation. Great person for historical analogies: how economies adjusted to past environmental shocks, pollution damages, coal/air-quality transitions, and what meta-analyses miss about institutional context. |
> | **Larasati Wulandari** | Organizer and likely junior researcher/graduate student. Treat as both intellectual contact and logistics anchor. Ask about dissertation/current project, datasets, and how the UofT group is thinking about climate-development links. |
> | **Benjamin Lauzon** | Lunch participant; likely junior scholar/graduate student. Good conversational mode: ask what he is working on and whether your GDP-impact meta-analysis intersects with his empirical methods or regional interests. |
> | **Ayaka Behro** | Likely junior researcher/graduate student. Same strategy: ask about current research, then offer links to climate impacts, adaptation, inequality, or data/modeling depending on her field. |
> | **Eduardo Souza-Rodrigues** | Environmental/development economist; strong work on land use, deforestation, conservation policy, especially Brazil/Amazon-style settings, using structural and empirical tools. Excellent overlap with climate damages through land-use feedbacks, policy counterfactuals, and heterogeneous impacts in developing regions. Ask about integrating biophysical models with economic behavioral response. |
> | **Hugo Cordeau** | Likely junior researcher/graduate student. Ask about research interests first; if quantitative, talk methods: meta-analysis, uncertainty, causal identification, damage functions, and how to communicate uncertainty without paralysis. |
> | **Christoph Semken** | UofT economist; likely relevant to environmental/resource/climate or applied micro circles. Since he is also at dinner, save broader agenda-setting questions for him: where the department’s climate-economics strengths are, who to collaborate with, and what seminar claims were most/least persuasive. |
> 
> **Seminar framing tip:** expect people to probe identification, publication bias, heterogeneity across income/temperature baselines, adaptation, and whether GDP damages are the right welfare object. A good recurring question to ask them: *“Where do you think macro damage functions most urgently need micro evidence?”*
